## Why it fails

- `@Observable` changes drive SwiftUI, so the model's state has to belong to the main actor.
- A `Task.detached` runs on no actor at all. Writing `model.notes = …` from it is a data race, and Swift 6 rejects it at compile time. Before Swift 6, `ObservableObject`/`@Published` only caught this at runtime, which is where "Publishing changes from background threads" came from.

## Fix

1. Isolate the model to the main actor.
2. Move only the disk I/O off the main thread.
3. Return `Sendable` values to the main actor and assign them there. Don't use `Task.detached`.

```swift
struct Note: Identifiable, Codable, Sendable {
    let id: UUID
    var title: String
    var body: String
}

// Disk I/O isolated away from the main actor
actor NotesStore {
    private let url: URL
    init(url: URL) { self.url = url }

    func loadNotes() throws -> [Note] {
        let data = try Data(contentsOf: url)
        return try JSONDecoder().decode([Note].self, from: data)
    }
}

@MainActor
@Observable
final class NotesModel {
    private(set) var notes: [Note] = []
    private(set) var loadError: (any Error)?
    private let store: NotesStore

    init(store: NotesStore) { self.store = store }

    func load() async {
        do {
            // Decoding runs on the store actor; we resume on the main actor
            notes = try await store.loadNotes()
        } catch {
            loadError = error
        }
    }
}

@main
struct NotesApp: App {
    @State private var model = NotesModel(
        store: NotesStore(url: .documentsDirectory.appending(path: "notes.json"))
    )

    var body: some Scene {
        WindowGroup {
            ContentView()
                .environment(model)
                .task { await model.load() }   // structured, cancellable, no detached Task
        }
    }
}
```

## Notes

- **Alternative to an actor:** a plain helper with a `@concurrent` function (`@concurrent func loadNotes() async throws -> [Note]`) also works. Use `@concurrent` on Swift 6.2 with "approachable concurrency" or default MainActor isolation turned on. Under those settings, a plain `nonisolated async` function runs on the caller's actor, so it would do the I/O on the main thread.
- **Don't silence the error** with `@unchecked Sendable`, `nonisolated(unsafe)`, or `DispatchQueue.main.async` inside the detached task. `await MainActor.run { model.notes = loaded }` compiles, but it's a workaround. Isolating the model is the real fix.
- **Saving:** do it the same way. The model passes a `[Note]` snapshot to `await store.save(notes)`.

