## Cause

SwiftUI reads `@Observable` properties on the main actor. Your detached `Task` runs on the global executor and writes `model.notes` from there. Swift 6 catches this at compile time as an isolation error. Before Swift 6 you only saw it at runtime as the "Publishing changes from background threads" warning.

## Fix

Isolate the model to the main actor. Do only the disk I/O off the main actor, then assign the result back on the main actor. You don't need `Task.detached`.

```swift
struct Note: Identifiable, Codable, Sendable {
    let id: UUID
    var title: String
    var body: String
}

@MainActor
@Observable
final class NotesModel {
    private(set) var notes: [Note] = []
    var loadError: Error?

    private let fileURL: URL
    init(fileURL: URL) { self.fileURL = fileURL }

    func load() async {
        do {
            notes = try await Self.readNotes(from: fileURL)   // assigned on the main actor
        } catch {
            loadError = error
        }
    }

    // Runs off the main actor. It only touches Sendable values (URL in, [Note] out).
    @concurrent   // Swift 6.2+. On 6.0/6.1, a plain `nonisolated` async func already runs off-actor.
    nonisolated private static func readNotes(from url: URL) async throws -> [Note] {
        let data = try Data(contentsOf: url)
        return try JSONDecoder().decode([Note].self, from: data)
    }
}
```

The App owns the model and starts the load from SwiftUI:

```swift
@main
struct NotesApp: App {
    @State private var model = NotesModel(fileURL: .documentsDirectory.appending(path: "notes.json"))

    var body: some Scene {
        WindowGroup {
            NotesListView()
                .environment(model)
                .task { await model.load() }   // cancelled automatically with the view
        }
    }
}

struct NotesListView: View {
    @Environment(NotesModel.self) private var model
    var body: some View {
        List(model.notes) { Text($0.title) }
    }
}
```

## Key points

- **Isolation:** Put `@MainActor` on the whole `@Observable` class, so every property write is main-actor isolated and checked by the compiler.
- **Background work:** Keep it in a `nonisolated` or `@concurrent` function that takes and returns `Sendable` values (`URL`, `[Note]`). Don't pass the model itself into background code.
- **Starting the load:** Prefer `.task { await model.load() }` over `Task.detached`, because it is tied to the view's lifetime and cancellation. If you need a task elsewhere, a plain `Task { await model.load() }` started from main-actor code is fine.
- **Avoid workarounds:** Don't silence the error with `DispatchQueue.main.async`, `@unchecked Sendable` or `nonisolated(unsafe)`. They hide the race instead of fixing it.
- **Heavy decoding:** If parsing a large file is slow, move it inside the same off-actor function, as shown above, so the main actor only does the final assignment.

You're on 5.1.0, so these are v5 APIs. The first screen has one bug (decoding still runs on the main thread). The second screen has two (it keeps `result.items` after the handler returns, and one exception closed the observer).

## 1. `.flowOn(Dispatchers.IO)` doesn't move the decode

On Android, Ditto runs observer handlers on its own scope, which is `MainScope()`. In 5.1.0, `observe()` is a `callbackFlow` that calls `collect(query) { send(transform(it)) }`. `flowOn` only moves the `callbackFlow` producer. The SDK still launches your transform on the main thread. So during a sync burst, every `jsonString()` and `decodeFromString` call runs on the UI thread, and a profiler will show the decode on `main`.

The transform is a `suspend` lambda, and the result stays open while it's suspended. So the fix is to switch threads inside the transform and drop `flowOn`. Don't add `.buffer()` or launch a coroutine per event. The SDK already applies back-pressure and merges changes, and `stateIn` keeps only the latest value.

## 2. Keeping `result.items` and the observer that went silent

- **Keeping `items`:** a `DittoQueryResult` and its items are closed as soon as the handler returns. Reading them later from the detail view gives `IllegalStateException: Query result item has already been closed.` Copy the data into plain Kotlin objects inside the handler.
- **One bad document stops all updates:** if an exception escapes a `registerObserver` handler, the SDK logs `Unhandled exception in observer event handler` and **closes the observer**. The `decodeFromString` call threw on the malformed document, so the observer was closed and never fired again. With `observe()`, the same exception fails the Flow instead. Decode each document separately and skip the ones that fail.

## Corrected Kotlin

```kotlin
private val carJson = Json { ignoreUnknownKeys = true }

// Runs on whatever dispatcher the caller is on; call it inside withContext(Dispatchers.IO).
private fun DittoQueryResult.toCars(): List<Car> =
    items.mapNotNull { item ->
        val raw = item.jsonString()                 // extract while the result is open
        runCatching { carJson.decodeFromString<Car>(raw) }
            .onFailure { Log.w("Cars", "Skipping malformed car doc: ${it.message}") }
            .getOrNull()
    }

class CarsViewModel(ditto: Ditto) : ViewModel() {
    val cars: StateFlow<List<Car>> = ditto.store
        .observe("SELECT * FROM cars") { result ->
            withContext(Dispatchers.IO) { result.toCars() }   // off main; result still open
        }
        .stateIn(viewModelScope, SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed(5_000), emptyList())
    // When viewModelScope is cancelled, the observer closes. No handle needed.
}

class CarDetailViewModel(private val ditto: Ditto, carId: String) : ViewModel() {
    private val _car = MutableStateFlow<Car?>(null)      // plain data only, never items
    val car: StateFlow<Car?> = _car

    private val observer = ditto.store.registerObserver(
        "SELECT * FROM cars WHERE _id = :id", mapOf("id" to carId)
    ) { result ->
        try {
            _car.value = withContext(Dispatchers.IO) { result.toCars().firstOrNull() }
        } catch (e: CancellationException) {
            throw e
        } catch (e: Exception) {
            // Don't let anything escape the handler, or the SDK closes the observer.
            Log.e("CarDetail", "Observer handler failed", e)
        }
    }

    override fun onCleared() = observer.close()           // registerObserver handles must be closed
}
```

Notes:
- If the detail screen only needs data that's already in the list, skip the second observer. Store the selected `_id` and look the car up in `cars`.
- A `try/catch` around the whole handler is still worth keeping even with per-item decoding, because any other exception would also close the observer.
- To spot malformed documents early, log them or count them. Silently dropping them hides data problems.

Your teammate's code has five bugs. Any one of them is enough to stop all delivery. This follows the local Ditto Data Streams guidance; I haven't changed or run anything. Data Streams is a preview API (Kotlin 5.1.0+), so each file that uses it needs `@file:OptIn(com.ditto.kotlin.PreviewDataStreams::class)`.

## What's wrong

1. **NGN is turned on too late.** Ditto reads NGN once, when the instance is created. Setting `Os.setenv(...)` after creating Ditto does nothing. On 5.2.0, pass it in `DittoConfig.systemParameters` instead (or call `setenv` before `DittoFactory.create`). This must be done on both devices.
2. **The topic name is invalid.** Topics must match `^[a-zA-Z0-9_ ]{1,15}$`. The dash in `"sensor-data"` makes the bind fail with `Failed to bind topic: Err(InvalidTopic)`. Use `sensor_data`.
3. **The two sides use different reliability.** The tablet binds Reliable and the phone connects Unreliable, so the connection is rejected (`Rejected(ConnectionRejected)`). Use `Unreliable` on both sides, which suits sensor frames where each reading replaces the last.
4. **The candidate is stored to open later.** A candidate is only valid inside the bind callback. Using it afterwards throws "Attempted to use a DittoStreamCandidate after it was closed". Meanwhile the phone's `connect` waits until it times out with `Rejected(HandshakeTimedOut)`. Open the candidate inside the callback, or call `take()` and open it while the connect is still pending.
5. **The phone connects inside the presence callback, and again for every frame.** Don't call `connect` from the presence callback. Copy the peer key out and connect once from your own coroutine, then reuse that one stream for every frame. A connect per frame means 50 handshakes a second, all overlapping. Also, the phone's `connect` block has to open its own candidate, because `connect` doesn't do that for you.

## Fixed code

```kotlin
// Both devices: create Ditto with NGN on, then start sync (presence needs it)
val ditto = DittoFactory.create(DittoConfig(
    databaseId = "my-app",
    connect = DittoConfig.Connect.SmallPeersOnly(),
    persistenceDirectory = dir,
    experimental = DittoConfig.Experimental(),
    encryptionKey = null,
    systemParameters = mapOf(
        "ngn_data_streams_enabled" to true,
        "ngn_sync_enabled" to false,          // keep document sync on the normal path
    ),
))
ditto.sync.start()
```

```kotlin
@file:OptIn(com.ditto.kotlin.PreviewDataStreams::class)
// Tablet (receiver)
class SensorReceiver(ditto: Ditto, private val sink: Channel<ByteArray>) : AutoCloseable {
    private val scope = CoroutineScope(SupervisorJob() + Dispatchers.Default)
    private val streams = ConcurrentHashMap<String, DittoStream>()
    private val acceptor = ditto.dataStreams.bindTopic(          // keep this reference alive
        topic = "sensor_data",
        reliability = DittoReliability.Unreliable,
    ) { candidate ->
        val peer = candidate.peerKeyString()
        val stream = candidate.open { inbound ->                 // open inside the callback
            sink.trySend(inbound.payload())                      // runs on the driver thread: hand off and return
        }
        streams.put(peer, stream)?.let { old -> scope.launch { old.closeSync() } }
        scope.launch { stream.waitUntilClosed(); streams.remove(peer, stream) }
    }
    override fun close() {
        acceptor.close()                                         // does NOT close streams that are already open
        val open = streams.values.toList(); streams.clear()
        scope.launch { open.map { launch { it.closeSync() } }.joinAll(); scope.cancel() }
    }
}
```

```kotlin
@file:OptIn(com.ditto.kotlin.PreviewDataStreams::class)
// Phone (sender): connect once from a coroutine, reuse the stream
suspend fun streamReadings(ditto: Ditto, tabletKey: String, readings: Flow<ByteArray>) {
    var backoff = 500L
    while (currentCoroutineContext().isActive) {
        val stream = try {
            ditto.dataStreams.connect(
                peer = tabletKey,                                // copied out of the presence callback
                topic = "sensor_data",
                reliability = DittoReliability.Unreliable,
                timeoutMs = 10_000,
            ) { candidate -> candidate.open() }                  // connect doesn't open it for you
        } catch (e: DittoException.DataStreamsException) {
            currentCoroutineContext().ensureActive()
            delay(backoff); backoff = minOf(backoff * 2, 10_000); continue   // early HandshakeTimedOut is normal
        }
        backoff = 500
        val max = stream.maxSendSize()
        try {
            coroutineScope {
                val closed = launch { stream.waitUntilClosed() }
                val sending = launch {
                    readings.collect { frame -> if (frame.size <= max) stream.sendAndForget(frame) }
                }
                select<Unit> { closed.onJoin {}; sending.onJoin {} }
                coroutineContext.cancelChildren()
            }
        } finally { withContext(NonCancellable) { stream.closeSync() } }
        // Streams never reconnect on their own: loop back and connect again
    }
}
```

In the presence observer, only find the tablet's `peer.peerKey`, store it, and start `streamReadings` in its own coroutine if it isn't already running.

Unreliable frames can be lost, arrive out of order or arrive twice, so put a sequence number or timestamp in each frame and drop stale ones. Nothing is written to the Ditto store, which matches your "don't store them" requirement.

