Here are the main ways to cut re-renders in a large list driven by Zustand, roughly in order of impact.

**1. Select narrowly and never subscribe to the whole store**
```ts
// ❌ re-renders on ANY store change
const { items, filter } = useStore();

// ✅ re-renders only when these slices change
const items = useStore((s) => s.items);
const filter = useStore((s) => s.filter);
```

**2. Use `useShallow` when a selector returns a new object or array**
```ts
import { useShallow } from 'zustand/react/shallow';

const { items, filter } = useStore(useShallow((s) => ({ items: s.items, filter: s.filter })));
const ids = useStore(useShallow((s) => s.items.map((i) => i.id)));
```
A selector that builds a new array or object (`map`, `filter`, `{...}`) fails the `Object.is` check every time. Without `useShallow` it re-renders on every update, and in v5 it can loop forever.

**3. Have the parent subscribe to IDs and each row subscribe to its own item**
This is the most effective fix for big lists:
```tsx
function List() {
  const ids = useStore(useShallow((s) => s.order)); // or s.items.map(i => i.id)
  return ids.map((id) => <Row key={id} id={id} />);
}

const Row = memo(function Row({ id }: { id: string }) {
  const item = useStore((s) => s.byId[id]);
  const toggle = useStore((s) => s.toggle); // actions are stable
  return <div onClick={() => toggle(id)}>{item.title}</div>;
});
```
When one item changes, only that row re-renders. The parent re-renders only when IDs are added, removed or reordered.

**4. Normalize state and update immutably but minimally**
- Store items as `byId: Record<string, Item>` plus `order: string[]`.
- When updating, replace only the changed item: `set((s) => ({ byId: { ...s.byId, [id]: { ...s.byId[id], done: true } } }))`. Other items keep their references, so their rows' selectors return the same value and those rows skip rendering.
- Don't rebuild every item on each update (for example `items.map(i => ({...i}))`).

**5. Keep props stable for `memo` rows**
- Pass primitives like `id` instead of freshly created objects.
- Get actions from the store, or wrap callbacks in `useCallback`, instead of writing inline lambdas that capture changing state.
- Use stable `key`s, never the array index for lists that change.

**6. Memoize derived data outside render**
Use `useMemo` for filtering or sorting, or keep the derived result in the store. Select the raw inputs and derive from them. Don't derive a new array inside the selector unless you use `useShallow` or a memoized selector.

**7. Read transient or high-frequency values without subscribing**
For things like scroll position or drag coordinates, use `useStore.getState()` inside handlers, or `useStore.subscribe(...)` with a ref. That way they never trigger renders.

**8. Virtualize the list**
With `@tanstack/react-virtual` or `react-window`, you only render visible rows, so the cost of any re-render stays bounded.

**9. Verify with measurements**
Use the React DevTools Profiler with "Highlight updates when components render" turned on to see which components re-render and why. Check that a single-item update now re-renders only one `Row`.

