## Cause

The UI reads `notes`, so it must only change on the main actor. A detached Task runs on a background executor. Swift 6 rejects the assignment at compile time. Before Swift 6, the same mistake showed up at runtime as the "Publishing changes from background threads" warning. Calling `DispatchQueue.main.async` would hide the symptom without fixing the design.

## Fix: keep the model on the main actor and do only the disk I/O in the background

```swift
struct Note: Codable, Identifiable, Sendable {
    let id: UUID
    var title: String
    var body: String
}

@MainActor
@Observable
final class NotesModel {
    var notes: [Note] = []
    var loadError: (any Error)?
    private(set) var isLoading = false

    private let fileURL: URL

    init(fileURL: URL = .documentsDirectory.appending(path: "notes.json")) {
        self.fileURL = fileURL
    }

    func load() async {
        isLoading = true
        defer { isLoading = false }
        do {
            // Runs in the background; the result comes back to the main actor.
            notes = try await Self.readNotes(from: fileURL)
        } catch {
            loadError = error
        }
    }

    // Not isolated to the main actor, and it touches no model state.
    // Only Sendable values go in (URL) and come out ([Note]).
    @concurrent  // Swift 6.2+: forces this to run off the caller's actor
    nonisolated private static func readNotes(from url: URL) async throws -> [Note] {
        let data = try Data(contentsOf: url)
        return try JSONDecoder().decode([Note].self, from: data)
    }
}
```

The `@concurrent` line depends on your Swift version:
- **Swift 6.2 with "Approachable Concurrency" / `NonisolatedNonsendingByDefault` on:** keep `@concurrent`. Without it, a `nonisolated async` function runs on the caller's actor, which here is the main actor.
- **Swift 6.0 or 6.1:** remove `@concurrent`. A `nonisolated async` function already runs on the global executor.
- **Either version:** you can instead keep the body synchronous and call it with `try await Task.detached { try Self.readNotesSync(url) }.value`.

