- The default setup is foreground-only. Continuous background sync needs a real native foreground service with `FOREGROUND_SERVICE`, `FOREGROUND_SERVICE_CONNECTED_DEVICE`, `foregroundServiceType="connectedDevice"` and a visible notification.

Having the permissions is necessary but may not be enough. If devices still don't find each other once everything is granted, look at the Bluetooth hardware and radio state next.

None of the installed skills cover this. They're all about Ditto, and nothing you mentioned involves Ditto, so this is standard Zustand and React advice.

### 1. Select narrow slices, never the whole store
```ts
// ❌ re-renders on ANY store change
const { items, filter } = useStore();

// ✅ re-renders only when `items` changes
const items = useStore((s) => s.items);
```

### 2. Use `useShallow` when a selector returns a new object or array
Zustand compares the selector result with `Object.is`. Returning `{a, b}` or `s.items.map(...)` creates a new reference every time, so the component re-renders on every update.
```ts
import { useShallow } from 'zustand/react/shallow';

const { filter, sort } = useStore(useShallow((s) => ({ filter: s.filter, sort: s.sort })));
const ids = useStore(useShallow((s) => s.items.map((i) => i.id)));
```

### 3. Have the parent subscribe to IDs and each row to its own item
```tsx
function List() {
  const ids = useStore(useShallow((s) => s.order)); // string[]
  return ids.map((id) => <Row key={id} id={id} />);
}

const Row = memo(function Row({ id }: { id: string }) {
  const item = useStore((s) => s.byId[id]); // only this row re-renders when its item changes
  return <div>{item.title}</div>;
});
```
- Store items normalized (`byId` plus `order`) and update them immutably. Only the changed item should get a new reference, so the other rows keep theirs.
- Use stable `key`s (IDs, not array indexes).

### 4. Keep props stable for memoized rows
- Don't pass inline objects or closures to `memo` rows. Pass IDs instead, and read actions from the store.
- Actions are stable references, so selecting one never causes a re-render: `const toggle = useStore((s) => s.toggle);`

### 5. Move derived or expensive work out of render
- If filtering or sorting a large array is expensive, memoize the result with `useMemo` over the selected inputs. Alternatively, keep the derived list in the store and update it only when its inputs change.
- Don't compute the filtered array inside a plain selector without `useShallow` or a custom equality function (`createWithEqualityFn` from `zustand/traditional`).

### 6. Read high-frequency state without subscribing
For values like scroll position or drag coordinates that don't need to drive rendering:
```ts
useEffect(() => useStore.subscribe((s) => { ref.current!.style.transform = ... }), []);
// or read on demand: useStore.getState().value
```

### 7. Virtualize the list
Use `@tanstack/react-virtual` or `react-window` so only the visible rows mount. With thousands of rows this often helps more than everything above.

### 8. Measure
- Use the React DevTools Profiler with "Highlight updates when components render" and the "Why did this render?" view.
- Check whether the parent list re-renders, which points to the selector, or every row does, which points to unstable props or references.

**Usual fix:** have the parent select `ids` with `useShallow`, make each row a `memo` component that selects its own item by ID, keep data normalized with immutable updates, and add virtualization.

