Here's the setup for a new iOS and Android app on `ditto_live` 5.x. It follows the local Ditto Flutter skill and I didn't run any of it.

## 1. Install

```bash
flutter pub add ditto_live
flutter pub add permission_handler   # Android runtime permissions for P2P
```

Use Flutter 3.24.5 or later. A new app gets the latest stable `ditto_live`; 5.2.0 is the newest version the guidance was checked against.

**iOS**: set `platform :ios, '15.0'` in `ios/Podfile`. Ditto only supports CocoaPods, so Flutter generates the Podfile on the first build. On 5.1.0 and later, simulator builds also need the `EXCLUDED_ARCHS` `post_install` fix from the skill's `references/setup.md`.

Add these to `ios/Runner/Info.plist`:

```xml
<key>NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription</key>
<string>Uses Bluetooth to sync with nearby devices.</string>
<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
<string>Uses the local network to sync with nearby devices.</string>
<key>NSBonjourServices</key>
<array><string>_http-alt._tcp.</string></array>
```

**Android**: in `android/app/build.gradle.kts`, set `minSdk = 24`. On 5.1.0 and later, add this to the root `android/build.gradle.kts`, above `evaluationDependsOn(":app")`. Without it, every APK build fails at `checkDebugAarMetadata`.

```kotlin
subprojects {
    afterEvaluate {
        if (name == "ditto_live") {
            extensions.configure<com.android.build.api.dsl.LibraryExtension>("android") {
                compileSdk = 36
            }
        }
    }
}
```

The plugin already declares the Bluetooth, Wi-Fi and location permissions in its manifest, so don't add them again. Your app only has to request them at runtime, before `sync.start()`.

## 2. `lib/main.dart`

```dart
import 'package:ditto_live/ditto_live.dart';
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
import 'package:permission_handler/permission_handler.dart';

const _license = String.fromEnvironment('DITTO_LICENSE');

const _runtimePermissions = <String, Permission>{
  'android.permission.BLUETOOTH_SCAN': Permission.bluetoothScan,
  'android.permission.BLUETOOTH_CONNECT': Permission.bluetoothConnect,
  'android.permission.BLUETOOTH_ADVERTISE': Permission.bluetoothAdvertise,
  'android.permission.NEARBY_WIFI_DEVICES': Permission.nearbyWifiDevices,
  'android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION': Permission.locationWhenInUse,
  'android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION': Permission.locationWhenInUse,
};

Future<void> main() async {
  WidgetsFlutterBinding.ensureInitialized();

  await Ditto.init(); // required on 5.0.x, harmless later (memoized)
  // Open once, before runApp. Small-peers-only is the default connect mode;
  // spelled out here. Use the SDK default persistence directory.
  final ditto = await Ditto.open(const DittoConfig(
    connect: DittoConfigConnectSmallPeersOnly(),
  ));

  try {
    ditto.setOfflineOnlyLicenseToken(_license); // throws if invalid

    // Android: request missing runtime permissions BEFORE starting sync
    // (DittoSyncPermissions is 5.1.0+; empty list on iOS).
    final perms = DittoSyncPermissions();
    final missing = await perms.missingPermissions();
    final requests = missing.map((n) => _runtimePermissions[n]).nonNulls.toSet();
    if (requests.isNotEmpty) await requests.toList().request();

    ditto.sync.start(); // P2P transports are on by default on iOS/Android
  } catch (_) {
    await ditto.close(); // don't leave the store directory locked
    rethrow;
  }

  // Subscribe so peers send you their tasks.
  ditto.sync.registerSubscription('SELECT * FROM tasks');

  // Insert a document.
  await ditto.store.execute(
    'INSERT INTO tasks DOCUMENTS (:task) ON ID CONFLICT DO UPDATE',
    arguments: {
      'task': {'_id': 'task-1', 'title': 'Buy milk', 'done': false},
    },
  );

  // First query: task titles as a List<String>.
  final result = await ditto.store.execute('SELECT title FROM tasks');
  final titles = result.items
      .map((item) => item.value['title'] as String)
      .toList(); // copy out of the Iterable
  debugPrint('titles: $titles');

  runApp(MaterialApp(
    home: Scaffold(body: ListView(children: [for (final t in titles) ListTile(title: Text(t))])),
  ));
}
```

Run it with the license kept off the command line:

```bash
flutter run --dart-define-from-file=ditto.json   # {"DITTO_LICENSE": "..."}
```

## Notes

- **Version:** these are the v5 calls (`Ditto.open(DittoConfig(...))`, `ditto.sync.start()`). Don't use the v4 forms `startSync()` or `Ditto.open(identity:)`.
- **One instance:** open Ditto once per process and close it only when the app exits, never in `State.dispose()`. Opening it again in the same process can fail with `File already locked` or hang.
- **5.0.x:** `DittoSyncPermissions` doesn't exist there, so request the permissions with `permission_handler` directly.
- **Live updates:** for a list that updates as peers sync, use `ditto.store.registerObserver('SELECT title FROM tasks')`. Attach one `observer.changes.listen(...)` from a repository class and copy `result.items` into a `ValueNotifier`. Don't use a `StreamBuilder` on `changes`: a rebuild throws "Stream has already been listened to." Cancel the listener, then the observer, while Ditto is still open.

Don't register it inside the composable. The local Kotlin SDK guidance says to put it in the screen's **ViewModel** as an `observe()` Flow, turn that into a `StateFlow` with `stateIn(viewModelScope, WhileSubscribed(…))`, and read it in Compose with `collectAsStateWithLifecycle()`. It also says never to create observers inside a composable body (`references/lifetimes.md:59-60`).

```kotlin
// ViewModel (SDK 5.2.0, com.ditto.kotlin.*)
class CarsViewModel(ditto: Ditto) : ViewModel() {
    val cars: StateFlow<List<Car>> =
        ditto.store.observe("SELECT * FROM cars") { result ->
            // Pull the data out inside the lambda. Result items close when it returns.
            withContext(Dispatchers.IO) {
                result.items.map { carJson.decodeFromString<Car>(it.jsonString()) }
            }
        }.stateIn(viewModelScope, SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed(5_000), emptyList())
}

// Composable
@Composable
fun CarsScreen(vm: CarsViewModel = viewModel()) {
    val cars by vm.cars.collectAsStateWithLifecycle()
    // render cars
}
```

How it stops:
- **Leaving the screen:** when the screen leaves composition (or its lifecycle drops below STARTED), nothing is collecting the Flow any more. After the 5-second `WhileSubscribed` grace period, the Ditto observer closes. It is created again if the screen comes back.
- **ViewModel cleared:** when the back-stack entry is popped, `viewModelScope` is cancelled, which also closes the observer. You don't need to call `close()` yourself.
- **Stop immediately:** use `SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed()` with no timeout. The 5 seconds exists so the observer survives configuration changes.

Other details:
- **Moving work off the main thread:** use `withContext` inside the transform as shown. On Android the handler runs on the main thread, and `.flowOn()` doesn't affect it.
- **If you must use `registerObserver`:** keep the returned handle and call `close()` on it in `ViewModel.onCleared()`. Nothing closes it for you.
- **Dependency:** `collectAsStateWithLifecycle()` needs `androidx.lifecycle:lifecycle-runtime-compose`.

Sources: `references/lifetimes.md`, `references/observers.md:270-288`, `references/concurrency.md:147-154`.

On iOS, three keys are required in `ios/Runner/Info.plist`. Missing local-network or Bonjour configuration can stop discovery even when everything else is correct.

## 1. Info.plist (`ios/Runner/Info.plist`)

```xml
<key>NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription</key>
<string>Uses Bluetooth to sync with nearby devices.</string>
<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
<string>Uses the local network to sync with nearby devices.</string>
<key>NSBonjourServices</key>
<array>
	<string>_http-alt._tcp.</string>
</array>
```

- **`_http-alt._tcp.` must keep its trailing dot.** If the array already lists other services, add this one to it rather than replacing them.
- **Write purpose text for your own app.** Don't describe local networking as GPS or location.
- **Missing Bluetooth text can crash the app.** iOS can terminate the app when the Bluetooth API is used without `NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription`.
- **Don't add `com.apple.developer.networking.multicast`.** Ordinary Bonjour doesn't need this restricted entitlement.
- **Background Bluetooth sync (optional):** add `bluetooth-central` and `bluetooth-peripheral` to `UIBackgroundModes`. iOS still decides when the app runs in the background, so continuous sync isn't guaranteed.
- **Nothing to request in Dart on iOS.** iOS shows the permission prompts itself, using these strings. On iOS, `DittoSyncPermissions().missingPermissions()` always returns an empty list, so it tells you nothing there.

Also check that your code doesn't turn off peer-to-peer. It's on by default on native, but code like `updateTransportConfig` setting `bluetoothLE` or `lan` to `isEnabled = false` disables it. Gate any such code with `kIsWeb`, not `defaultTargetPlatform`.

