**How it works:**
- **Watching:** `initState` starts `ditto.presence.observe(...)`. It reports the current peers right away, then reports again whenever a peer appears, leaves or changes how it's connected.
- **Stopping:** `dispose()` calls `_observer.stop()` when the screen closes. You have to call `stop()` (not `cancel`). Just dropping the reference leaves the callback running until Ditto closes.
- **Connection type:** A peer's connection list can include links between two other peers, so the screen keeps only the links where one end is this device. If none are left, the peer is reached through other peers and shows "Via other peers".
- **Multicast:** The `switch` includes `multicast`, which exists from 5.1.0 on. If you ever drop to 5.0.x, remove that line or the code won't compile.
- **Limits:** There's no distance or signal-strength value in v5. Flutter Web has no peer-to-peer, so on web the list only shows peers connected through a server.
- **Hot restart:** Hot restart (`R`) while this screen is open might crash the app. This hasn't been tested for presence observers, but the same mechanism has crashed other observers. Hot reload is fine.

Here's the screen. The State owns one observer and one sync subscription, re-registers both if `color` changes, and cancels everything in `dispose()`.

```dart
import 'dart:async';

import 'package:ditto_live/ditto_live.dart';
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class CarsByColorScreen extends StatefulWidget {
  const CarsByColorScreen({super.key, required this.ditto, required this.color});

  final Ditto ditto;
  final String color;

  @override
  State<CarsByColorScreen> createState() => _CarsByColorScreenState();
}

class _CarsByColorScreenState extends State<CarsByColorScreen> {
  static const _query = 'SELECT * FROM cars WHERE color = :color';

  SyncSubscription? _subscription;
  StoreObserver? _observer;
  StreamSubscription<QueryResult>? _changes;
  List<Map<String, dynamic>> _cars = const [];

  @override
  void initState() {
    super.initState();
    _start(widget.color);
  }

  @override
  void didUpdateWidget(CarsByColorScreen oldWidget) {
    super.didUpdateWidget(oldWidget);
    if (oldWidget.color != widget.color || oldWidget.ditto != widget.ditto) {
      _stop();
      _cars = const [];
      _start(widget.color);
    }
  }

  void _start(String color) {
    final args = {'color': color};
    // Pull matching docs from peers (subscriptions take no LIMIT/ORDER BY).
    _subscription =
        widget.ditto.sync.registerSubscription(_query, arguments: args);
    // No onChange: this State holds the single `changes` listener.
    _observer = widget.ditto.store.registerObserver(_query, arguments: args);
    _changes = _observer!.changes.listen((result) {
      if (!mounted) return;
      // Copy out of the result; never keep the Iterable in state.
      final cars = result.items
          .map((item) => Map<String, dynamic>.from(item.value))
          .toList(growable: false);
      setState(() => _cars = cars);
    });
  }

  void _stop() {
    // dispose() can't await. Ditto must still be open here.
    final changes = _changes;
    if (changes != null) unawaited(changes.cancel());
    _observer?.cancel(); // cancelling only the listener leaves the observer running
    _subscription?.cancel();
    _changes = null;
    _observer = null;
    _subscription = null;
  }

  @override
  void dispose() {
    _stop(); // don't call ditto.close() here; the app owns Ditto
    super.dispose();
  }

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      appBar: AppBar(title: Text('${widget.color} cars')),
      body: _cars.isEmpty
          ? const Center(child: Text('No cars'))
          : ListView.builder(
              itemCount: _cars.length,
              itemBuilder: (context, i) {
                final car = _cars[i];
                return ListTile(
                  title: Text('${car['make'] ?? car['_id']}'),
                  subtitle: Text('${car['_id']}'),
                );
              },
            ),
    );
  }
}
```

**Why it's built this way:**
- **No `StreamBuilder` over `observer.changes`.** That stream allows only one listener, so a rebuild throws `Stream has already been listened to.` and the observer is left running. The State listens once instead.
- **No `onChange:` callback.** On 5.0.x through 5.2.0, results also pile up in the `changes` stream when nothing listens to it, so memory grows. Using `changes.listen` alone avoids that.
- **Cleanup in `dispose()`.** It starts the listener's `cancel()` without awaiting it, because `dispose()` can't await. Then it calls `observer.cancel()` and `subscription.cancel()`. You need the explicit `observer.cancel()` because a V1 observer keeps running if you only cancel its listener.
- **`if (!mounted) return;`** stops a late callback from calling `setState` on a screen that has already closed.
- **`didUpdateWidget`** tears down and re-registers the query if the parent rebuilds with a different color.
- **The subscription is optional.** Drop `registerSubscription` if another part of the app already subscribes to `cars`. Keep it if this screen should pull matching cars from peers.

